## Section 3 — Worker Incentive Model: Purity-Linked Compensation

### 3.1 System Architecture

```
[IoT Load Cell on Sorting Bin] → [Edge Gateway (MQTT)] → [Cloud Aggregator]
                                                                    │
[AI-EcoTrack DPP Material Values] ──────────────────────────────────┤
                                                                    │
[Live Commodity Price Feed (LME / SCRAP API)] ──────────────────────┤
                                                                    ▼
                                              [Incentive Calculation Engine]
                                                                    │
                                                                    ▼
                                              [Technician Incentive Ledger]
                                              [Shift Dashboard + Mobile Alert]
```

---

### 3.2 Purity Classification & Grade Mapping

| Purity Grade | Definition | XRF / Sensor Confirmation | Point Multiplier |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A** | Single-alloy, uncontaminated, ≥98% base metal | Required for Ni, Ti, Cu alloys | 3.0× |
| **B** | Mixed-grade but identifiable alloy family | Required for stainless families | 1.8× |
| **C** | Mixed polymer or composite stream | Visual inspection | 0.5× |
| **REJECT** | Cross-contaminated (e.g., SS + carbon steel in same bin) | Auto-flagged by density sensor | −2.0× (penalty) |

---

### 3.3 Incentive Calculation Formula

**Daily Incentive (DI) per technician:**

```
DI = Σ [ (mass_kg × commodity_price_usd_per_kg × purity_multiplier) × shift_bonus_coeff ]
         for each bin_closing_event in shift

Where:
  commodity_price_usd_per_kg  = Live LME spot price pulled at shift-end (15-min delayed)
  purity_multiplier           = Grade A: 0.018 | Grade B: 0.010 | Grade C: 0.002
                                (fraction of recovery value paid as incentive)
  shift_bonus_coeff           = 1.0 (standard)
                              = 1.25 (if zero REJECT events in shift)
                              = 1.50 (if ≥95% of bins are Grade A for full week)
```

**Example Calculation — Single shift:**

| Bin | Material | Mass (kg) | LME Price ($/kg) | Grade | Multiplier | Payout ($) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| BIN-SS316-A-07 | 316L SS | 48.2 | 1.42 | A | 0.018 | $1.23 |
| BIN-NICKELALLOY-B-02 | Inconel 625 | 12.5 | 18.50 | B | 0.010 | $2.31 |
| BIN-CUALLOY-A-03 | C110 Copper | 22.0 | 8.80 | A | 0.018 | $3.48 |
| BIN-POLYMER-C-11 | Mixed PTFE | 8.3 | 0.08 | C | 0.002 | $0.00 |
| **Subtotal** | | | | | | **$7.02** |
| Zero-reject bonus (×1.25) | | | | | | **$8.78** |

A technician processing high-value alloys can realistically earn **$150–$300/month** in incentive pay on top of base wages in a mid-sized manufacturing facility — without requiring any subjective performance assessment.

---

### 3.4 Anti-Gaming Controls

| Risk | Control Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Inflating mass by adding debris | Load cell delta cross-checked against DPP mass estimates (±15% tolerance) |
| Falsely claiming Grade A | XRF spot-check protocol: 1 in 10 bins audited; penalty = 2× incentive clawback |
| Bin switching between technicians | RFID-tagged bin assigned to technician at scan time; immutable in DPP audit trail |
| Dumping hazardous into recovery bins | Inline spectrometry sensor on high-value bins; hazmat detection triggers bin lock |

---

### 3.5 Operational Dashboard KPIs

| KPI | Target | Alert Threshold |
|---|---|---|
| Shift Purity Index (SPI) | ≥ 0.88 WRI | < 0.75 |
| Grade A Bin Rate | ≥ 70% | < 50% |
| REJECT Event Rate | ≤ 2% of bins | > 5% |
| Recovery Value Capture Rate | ≥ 92% of theoretical DPP value | < 80% |
| Avg Disassembly Time per Part | ≤ target (by part class) | > 150% of target |

---

## Section 4 — Aerospace Adaptation Strategy

### 4.1 Context: Manufacturing vs. Aerospace MRO

The core AI-EcoTrack architecture is valid for aerospace, but the **compliance environment, material families, and traceability requirements** are fundamentally different from general manufacturing.

| Dimension | General Manufacturing | Aerospace MRO |
|---|---|---|
| Regulatory Framework | OSHA, REACH, RoHS | FAA Order 8130.3, AS9100D, EASA Part 145 |
| Traceability Depth | Component-level | Serialized sub-component level (fasteners, bearings) |
| Material Families | Carbon/stainless steel, cast iron, PTFE | Ti-6Al-4V, Inconel 718, CFRP, Al 7075-T6 |
| Part Retirement Trigger | Hours / Corrosion / Cost | Calendar life, cycle count, stress exposure |
| Disassembly Authority | Factory technician | Licensed AME / A&P Mechanic (FAA Part 65) |
| Data Integrity Standard | Internal ERP | CAMP / AMOS MRO software + FAA Form 337 |

---

### 4.2 VLM Adaptation for Aerospace Parts

**Training corpus additions required:**

| Dataset | Source | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Boeing CMM image sets | Negotiated OEM access | Part identification for high-life components |
| Airbus IPC (Illustrated Parts Catalog) | Airbus MRO portal | Subcomponent mapping and serialization |
| NIST Aerospace Alloy Database | NIST MML | Material inference for Ti, Ni, Al aerospace grades |
| FAA Suspected Unapproved Parts (SUP) database | FAA.gov | Counterfeit part detection visual signatures |
| CFRP delamination image library | NASA Technical Reports | Surface condition grading for composite panels |

**New VLM output fields for aerospace:**

```json
{
  "aerospace_extensions": {
    "part_number_faa": "NAS6703U4D",
    "airworthiness_tag": {
      "form_8130_3_present": true,
      "tag_id": "8130-2024-AA-004421",
      "certifying_authority": "EASA_Part_21J"
    },
    "cycle_count_visible": false,
    "suspected_unapproved_part_risk": 0.04,
    "life_limit_status": {
      "calendar_life_remaining_days": 0,
      "total_cycles_estimated": 12440,
      "life_limit_cycles": 12000,
      "status": "life_expired"
    },
    "scrap_mandatory": true,
    "scrap_mutilation_required": true
  }
}
```

> [!CAUTION]
> Life-expired aerospace parts are subject to **mandatory scrap mutilation** under FAA Advisory Circular AC 20-62E. The DPP must record the mutilation event and the part must never re-enter the parts supply chain. The AI-EcoTrack system must enforce a hard `scrap_mandatory` flag that prevents routing to any refurbishment stream.

---

### 4.3 Aerospace DPP Lifecycle Stages

```
[New Part Manufacture] → Form 8130-3 issued → DPP Created
        │
        ▼
[In-Service (Aircraft)] → Cycle count incremented per flight log
        │
        ▼
[MRO Event] → VLM scan → Condition graded → Repair or Return-to-Service cert
        │
        ▼
[Life Limit Reached] → Mandatory removal → DPP lifecycle_stage = "life_expired"
        │
        ├──→ [Scrap for Material Recovery] → Ti/Ni alloy to certified scrap yard
        │       DPP records: mutilation_confirmed = true, mutilation_witness_id
        │
        └──→ [Serviceable Used Part — if life remaining] → DPP transferred to new operator
```

---

### 4.4 Material Recovery Value — Aerospace Alloy Specifics

The economics of aerospace material recovery are substantially higher than general manufacturing:

| Alloy | Typical Component | Recovery Grade | LME/SCRAP Price ($/kg) | vs. Carbon Steel |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Ti-6Al-4V | Fan blades, fasteners, landing gear | A | $28–$35 | 35–44× |
| Inconel 718 | Turbine discs, combustor casings | A | $20–$28 | 25–35× |
| Al 7075-T6 | Wing spars, fuselage frames | A | $1.80–$2.20 | 2.3–2.8× |
| CFRP panels | Nacelles, control surfaces | C (recyclate) | $0.50–$1.20 | 0.6–1.5× |
| Hastelloy X | Combustor liners | B | $14–$18 | 18–23× |

**Strategic implication:** A single retired CFM56 turbine disc (Inconel 718, ~120 kg) represents **$2,400–$3,360** in certified scrap recovery value. Material purity assurance at the aerospace scrap yard level directly impacts smelter procurement agreements and justifies the full AI-EcoTrack deployment cost in a single part.

---

### 4.5 Compliance Integration Layer

To operate in an aerospace MRO context, AI-EcoTrack requires an additional **Compliance API Layer**:

```
[AI-EcoTrack DPP Engine]
        │
        ├──→ [CAMP / AMOS MRO Sync]  ← Bi-directional: pulls cycle counts, pushes scan events
        │
        ├──→ [FAA DragonWave / Aviation Registry API]  ← Validates part numbers vs. TSO/PMA status
        │
        ├──→ [AS9100D QMS Integration]  ← Pushes disassembly events as quality records
        │
        └──→ [Certified Scrap Yard API (e.g., Kitchener Clark)]  ← Pushes DPP + mutilation cert
```

---

## Appendix: Key Performance Metrics Summary

| Metric | Target | Measurement Method |
|---|---|---|
| VLM Part Classification Accuracy | ≥ 96% | Validation set of 5,000 labeled industrial parts |
| Safety Protocol Coverage Score | ≥ 99% | Ground-truth safety checklist vs. retrieved steps |
| Material Inference Accuracy | ≥ 91% | XRF verification of VLM material calls |
| DPP Completeness Rate | 100% | Every scanned part must generate a closed DPP |
| Weighted Recyclability Index (WRI) | ≥ 0.85 facility avg | Calculated from DPP bill-of-materials |
| Recovery Value Capture Rate | ≥ 92% | Actual scrap revenue / theoretical DPP value |
| Grade A Bin Rate | ≥ 70% | IoT sensor + audit log |
| Aerospace: Zero SUP False Negative Rate | 100% | All suspected unapproved parts must be flagged |

